;; naming-target-handle-surface.wat — the naming decision for arc-170's capability-circuit
;; handle-unification stone. A bracket is handed ONE Vector of service handles; from each it
;; must GRANT a worker's pid AND get the service's ADDRESS to dial. Today a common surface
;; (:wat::capability::Grantable) carries grant/revoke and makes a heterogeneous Vector of
;; different services' handles HOMOGENEOUS. We are ADDING an address hook so the ONE vec
;; carries BOTH capabilities (grant + dial), and the bracket needs nothing else.
;;
;; TWO names are in question. Weigh which KEEPS ITS PROMISE (says what it is) to a reader
;; with no context.

;; ── DECISION 1: the address method added to the surface ──────────────────────────────────
;; It returns the service's live coordinate — the pure, brute-forceable location a worker
;; dials (connect' : Address' -> Peer'). Type-erased to bare :wat::kernel::Address'.
;; Anchors on the disk:
;;   - the concrete handle already exposes this as a struct accessor `<fqdn>::Handle/addr`
;;   - arc-278 R32 (QVANTVMVIS PROCVL IDEM NEXVS): "a service is a surface at a COORDINATE";
;;     the builder crowned `coordinate` ("that is a wonderful word") for the Address'.
;; Candidates (which one says "the location I dial", not a mechanism/verb?):
;;   coordinate  — R32's word; the address IS the coordinate; a pure location (recommended?)
;;   addr        — matches the existing Handle/addr accessor; terse
;;   address     — full form of addr
;;   reach       — reads as a verb / an act, not the location
;;   where       — vague
;;   dial-addr   — names the consumer's use, not the thing

;; ── DECISION 2: the surface's name ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; Today: :wat::capability::Grantable — named for grant/revoke ONLY. With the address hook it
;; now carries the WHOLE owner-facing capability of a service handle: grant a pid + revoke a
;; pid + hand out the coordinate to dial. Is "Grantable" still honest (it names one of three
;; features), or does the broader thing want a broader name?
;; Anchors:
;;   - every service's <fqdn>::Handle satisfies it (the defservice macro auto-emits extend-type)
;;   - it is the thing an OWNER holds and hands to a bracket to say "workers may use this service"
;;   - R28/R38: surfaces are wat's interface mechanism (supersede Clojure protocols)
;; Candidates (which one says what a service handle IS to its owner?):
;;   Grantable      — keep it; grant is the load-bearing verb, addr rides along (honest-enough?)
;;   ServiceHandle  — what it literally is: the owner's handle to a service
;;   Capability     — the ocap term; the handle IS the capability (grant + revoke + reach)
;;   Reachable      — names the new addr hook, loses grant
;;   Dialable       — names dialing, loses grant/revoke
;;   Handle'        — the primed-handle convention

;; The surface as it would read WITH the address method (name TBD by this cast):
(:wat::core::defsurface :wat::capability::Grantable :nature :wat::core::Struct
  :features
  [(grant      [self <- :wat::capability::Grantable  pids <- (:wat::core::Vector :wat::core::i64)] -> :wat::core::nil)
   (revoke     [self <- :wat::capability::Grantable  pids <- (:wat::core::Vector :wat::core::i64)] -> :wat::core::nil)
   (coordinate [self <- :wat::capability::Grantable] -> :wat::kernel::Address')])   ;; ← DECISION 1 name shown as `coordinate`

;; The user form it enables (a bracket handed ONE vec of handles):
;;   (:wat::spawn::process/uses (:wat::core::Vector :wat::capability::Grantable kvh eh))
;; the bracket, per handle: (grant h [pid]) + (coordinate h) -> ship -> worker dials.
